 "rulebook": "FIG Apparatus Norms (men's vault): the vault table 1.35 m high, its top 1.20 m long and 0.95 m wide; a run-up of 25 m, 1 m wide; a springboard 1.20 m by 0.60 m, 0.22 m high at its high end; landing mats 6 m by 2.5 m, 0.20 m thick. FIG Code of Points for Men's Artistic Gymnastics, the vault section, for what makes a vault invalid: no support of a hand on the table, a foot on the table, not landing feet first",
 "deviations": [
  "Apparatus Norms (springboard): the board is a rigid plate hinged at its low end on one spring of 120000.0 N m/rad (83 kN/m at its high end with the default) with light damping, not a leaf-spring board with coil springs; its elasticity is not FIG-tested (the norm is a drop test on the real board)",
  "Code of Points (springboard position): the board stands at a fixed place, its high end 0.5 m before the table, instead of where the gymnast puts it (nobody sets up the venue for the robot)",
  "Code of Points (the vault): any passage over the table with hand support counts, with no vault number, no required take-off from the board and no execution score: the family asks whether the vault is valid, not how well it is done",
  "Code of Points (landing): judged to a stand of 1.0 s on the mat with nothing but the feet on it; the landing mat's corridor lines are not judged",
  "Apparatus Norms (run-up): the robot starts at the far end of the 25.0 m run-up (the gymnast may start nearer); the table's safety collar and the mat around the board are left out"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Vault over the vault table at the end of the run-up: go over it with only your hands touching it, land on your feet on the landing mat and stand.",
  "rules": "The vault table's top is 1.35 m above the floor, 1.2 m long (along +x, the way you face) and 0.95 m wide, on a pedestal; its near edge is 25.0 m ahead of where your pelvis starts, centred on your line. A springboard stands before it: a board 1.2 m long and 0.6 m wide (20.0 kg), hinged at its low near end and rising to 0.22 m at its high end, which is 0.5 m before the table; it rests on a spring of 120000.0 N m/rad about the hinge (damping 300.0 N m s/rad) and gives way under load down to the floor. The landing mat begins at the table's far edge: 6.0 m long, 2.5 m wide, 0.2 m thick, softer than the floor (contact time constant 0.04 s). A valid vault, in order: at least one hand touches the table's top surface (a contact within 0.02 m of the top plane; a hand against a side of the table does not count); your pelvis passes the table's far edge; your feet touch the mat; you stand on the mat for 1.0 s without a break (only your feet on it, your torso within 20.0 degrees of vertical, your pelvis moving no faster than 0.35 m/s). You fail the moment: any part of you other than your hands (the wrist links and the palms; not the forearms) touches the table or its pedestal; your pelvis is between the table's near and far edges without being higher than its top and within its width (you went under, beside or round it); your pelvis passes the far edge before a hand has touched the top; after your pelvis has passed the far edge, any part of you touches the floor, or a part other than your feet touches the mat before the stand is complete. Before the table there is no fall rule: any part of you may touch the floor and the springboard, and using the springboard is not required. The attempt ends after 80 s of robot time.",
  "venue": "You stand at the start of the run-up, a strip 1.0 m wide that leads straight ahead (+x) to the springboard and the table; the board's low end is 23.3 m ahead of you and its high end 24.5 m. The table's top is higher than your shoulders' joints are when you stand (about 1.47 m is the height of your shoulders, 1.03 m that of your pelvis). Table, pedestal and mat are fixed; the board moves on its spring.",
  "difficulty": "The table is 1.35 m high and the robot has no running policy: its walk reaches about 0.65 m/s and its best standing jump lifts it far less than a metre, so the 67 kg body has to be carried over the table by a take-off and arm support that nobody has built for it. It must then come down feet first from above 1.35 m and absorb the landing without a hand on the mat."
 },
